From 2bc2e85870c3a91d1927b9b6ef830528f7eb38ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jay Leaton <58495321+jayleaton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 06:30:24 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 17/60] TP RoCE: run_hosts.sh gives NCCL the RoCE picker's
 ports when neither TF_TP_ROCE_HCA nor NCCL_IB_HCA is set

- `tf-tp-test hca` (host only, no GPU): both ranks run the RoCE pick over the bootstrap, the same rule the wire uses,
  and each prints its own devices as `NCCL_IB_HCA=name,...` (no GIDs). It exits 1 with the reason when the host has
  no usable port or no port on a /24 the peer shares, so no rank falls back to its first ports for NCCL.
- run_hosts.sh runs it when neither variable is set, exports the names for every step and logs each rank's pick;
  a failed or malformed `hca` stops the runner (exit 3) with the reason; `hca` is capped at TF_TP_PLAN_TIMEOUT + 31 s, so
  its own reason (no peer joined in time, port in use, the peer closed, a bootstrap error) comes first. An explicit NCCL_IB_HCA, an explicit
  TF_TP_ROCE_HCA (NCCL_IB_HCA derived from it) or both keep their behaviour; the many-devices warning is gone.
  Both hosts use the same mode: with one naming its ports and the other not, the latter's `hca` finds no peer and
  stops after TF_TP_PLAN_TIMEOUT with that reason (README).
- roce_pick: `select` / `selectFrom` return how the devices were chosen (`resolve` is unchanged for the wire),
  `joinNames` and `hcaLine` give the NCCL list. Host tests: the port scan over a stub verbs table (state, Ethernet,
  the IPv4 RoCE v2 GID index), then both ranks through a real bootstrap on the two hosts #636's receipt reports
  (modelled, documentation addresses), an explicit rank, and a host without ports.

(cherry picked from commit 989ca7e6578309da169d94f55b9ffd17541b1bf1)
